:: StoryTitle
Thistledown

:: StoryData
{
  "ifid": "<generated by Twine>",
  "format": "Inkjot",
  "format-version": "0.6.0",
  "start": "arrival"
}

:: Inklink [config]
(((
Ex 5: a solo story-game, written to test v0.6's rules. A draft: it uses
structured facts and rules, stratified `not`, two sets of truth (the
garden and your almanac), rules you learn, seasons, a grid, `recipe`,
`assert` / `retract`, and `{known about}`, none of which `inkjot check`
0.5 accepts.

The garden runs on every rule, applied to what is true. Your almanac runs
only on the rules you know, applied only to what you have seen. You plant
by the almanac. When it is wrong, you have learned something.

The story stays light: one fragment for each gnome who comes to trust you.
)))
kind: story-game
tags: cozy ~ garden ~ deduction ~ folklore
ages: 9..
mode: solo
pov: you
clock: 0..=8 ~ shown as "month"
~ season 3 ~ seasons spring, summer, autumn
start: cottage
default moves: walk

character you: "the new gardener" ~ playable ~ custom
resource daylight: 6 ~ per character ~ refill each chronon ~ shown as "hours of daylight"
resource hints: 3

map garden: 3 by 3 ~ shows visited
~ nw | n | ne
~ w  | c | e
~ sw | s | se
place nw: "the north-west bed"
place n: "the north bed"
place ne: "the north-east bed"
place w: "the west bed"
place c: "the middle bed"
place e: "the east bed"
place sw: "the south-west bed"
place s: "the south bed"
place se: "the south-east bed"
place cottage: "the cottage"
place shed: "the potting shed"
place hedge: "the gap in the hedge"
place lane: "the lane to Underwick"
way back_door: cottage - s
way shed_door: shed - sw
way hedge_gap: hedge - e
way gate: lane - cottage

act walk: cost 0 ~ steps 1
act examine: cost 1
act observe: cost 2 ~ watches here
act sow: cost 2
act water: cost 1
act leave: cost 1
act dig: cost 3 ~ loud
act make: cost 2

stat green_thumb: 0..=5 ~ shown as "green thumb"
relation trust: you, each gnome ~ 0..=3 ~ shown as "trust"

state lantern_lit: no ~ belongs to world
state nibbled: nothing
state offering_taken: no

(((
The truth of the garden, as facts. Every fact's meaning holds in the
garden from the start; it holds in your almanac only once you learn it.
)))
verb soil ~ one value
verb gets ~ one value

fact soil_nw: "The north-west bed is sandy, and drains before you have finished watering it." ~ means {{ nw soil sand }}
fact soil_n: "The north bed is good dark loam." ~ means {{ n soil loam }}
fact soil_ne: "The north-east bed is loam, a little stony." ~ means {{ ne soil loam }}
fact soil_w: "The west bed is clay, cold and slow." ~ means {{ w soil clay }}
fact soil_c: "The middle bed is the best loam in the garden, as if someone fed it for years." ~ means {{ c soil loam }}
fact soil_e: "The east bed is sand, from the old path." ~ means {{ e soil sand }}
fact soil_sw: "The south-west bed is clay." ~ means {{ sw soil clay }}
fact soil_s: "The south bed is loam." ~ means {{ s soil loam }}
fact soil_se: "The south-east bed is clay, and holds the rain." ~ means {{ se soil clay }}

fact oak_east: "Mr. Lowe’s oak hangs over the hedge onto the east beds." ~ means {{ oak shades e }}
fact oak_ne: "The oak reaches the north-east bed too." ~ means {{ oak shades ne }}
fact oak_se: "And the south-east, when it’s in full leaf." ~ means {{ oak shades se }}
fact red_sky: "A red sky at evening means rain next month." ~ means {{ red_sky warns rain }}

(((
What each plant wants. Printed on the seed packets, so known from the start.
)))
fact rose_wants: "Rose: loam, full sun, regular water." ~ known ~ means {{ rose likes loam }}, {{ rose wants sun }}
fact lavender_wants: "Lavender: sand, full sun. Hates wet feet." ~ known ~ means {{ lavender likes sand }}, {{ lavender wants sun }}
fact foxglove_wants: "Foxglove: clay or loam, shade." ~ known ~ means {{ foxglove likes clay }}, {{ foxglove likes loam }}, {{ foxglove wants shade }}
fact marigold_wants: "Marigold: anything, sun." ~ known ~ means {{ marigold likes sand }}, {{ marigold likes loam }}, {{ marigold likes clay }}, {{ marigold wants sun }}
fact pumpkin_wants: "Pumpkin: loam, sun, a great deal of water." ~ known ~ means {{ pumpkin likes loam }}, {{ pumpkin wants sun }}

(((
Your almanac shows what it knows about a bed with `{known about: here}`:
the facts you have learned, and what the rules you know derive from them.
)))

item rose_seed: "a twist of rose seed" ~ seed ~ held by you ~ grows rose
item lavender_seed: "a paper of lavender seed" ~ seed ~ held by you ~ grows lavender
item foxglove_seed: "a pinch of foxglove seed" ~ seed ~ held by you ~ grows foxglove
item marigold_seed: "an envelope of marigold seed" ~ seed ~ held by you ~ grows marigold
item pumpkin_seed: "three fat pumpkin seeds" ~ seed ~ held by you ~ grows pumpkin
item mirror_lily_seed: "a seed like a pearl" ~ seed ~ held by pip ~ grows mirror_lily
item shadebell_seed: "a seed like a drop of ink" ~ seed ~ held by sorrel ~ grows shadebell
item thundercap_seed: "a pinch of grey spores in a walnut shell" ~ seed ~ held by mallow ~ grows thundercap
item moonbloom_seed: "a seed that is cold to the touch" ~ seed ~ grows moonbloom
item watering_can: "a dented watering can" ~ tool ~ held by you
item mattock: "Hester’s mattock" ~ tool ~ in shed
item compost: "a barrow of black compost" ~ in shed
item jam_jar: "an empty jam jar" ~ in cottage
item candle_end: "the end of a candle" ~ in cottage
item lantern: "a jam-jar lantern" ~ watches here when lantern_lit
item honey: "a spoon of honey in a twist of paper" ~ offering ~ in cottage
item buttons: "a tin of odd buttons" ~ offering ~ in cottage
item crusts: "bread crusts" ~ offering ~ in cottage
item thimble: "a silver thimble" ~ offering ~ in shed
item almanac: "Hester’s almanac" ~ held by you
item thistle: "a thistle taller than you are" ~ fixed ~ in c ~ watches here ~ means {{ thistle in c }}

recipe lantern: jam_jar, candle_end ~ make ~ "A jar, a candle stub, a bit of wire for a handle."

npc sorrel: "Old Sorrel" ~ like gnome ~ tender ~ likes honey ~ aka "Hester"
~ lore sorrel_lore ~ fragment frag_sorrel ~ gift shadebell_seed
npc pip: "Pip" ~ like gnome ~ tender ~ likes buttons
~ lore pip_lore ~ fragment frag_pip ~ gift mirror_lily_seed
npc mallow: "Mallow" ~ like gnome ~ nibbler ~ likes crusts
~ lore mallow_lore ~ fragment frag_mallow ~ gift thundercap_seed
npc nettle: "Nettle" ~ like gnome ~ nibbler ~ likes thimble
~ lore nettle_lore ~ fragment frag_nettle
npc lowe: "Mr. Crispin Lowe" ~ rival ~ in hedge ~ moves none
npc marmalade: "Marmalade, Mr. Lowe’s cat" ~ like cat

fact pip_lore: "Pip’s lore: a marigold keeps the slugs off its neighbours. A mirror-lily throws the sun next door." ~ lore
fact sorrel_lore: "Sorrel’s lore: rain in clay brings the slugs, whatever the almanac says. And a shadebell darkens all around it." ~ lore
fact mallow_lore: "Mallow’s lore: a thundercap waters its own bed and its neighbours, rain or no rain." ~ lore
fact nettle_lore: "Nettle’s lore: the moonbloom wants a blessed bed and the dark. Nothing else will do." ~ lore
fact fair_notice: "The Underwick Harvest Fair. Prizes for any marvel grown in good health, and for the biggest pumpkin, properly fed." ~ notice

fact frag_pip: "“Hester wasn’t sad when she went,” says Pip. “She was laughing. We all were.”" ~ fragment
fact frag_mallow: "“Nettle wanted the thistle,” says Mallow. “Nettle always wants. Hester said no, and Nettle bit her. She didn’t mind. She said gardeners get bitten.”" ~ fragment
fact frag_nettle: "“I wanted the thistledown for myself,” says Nettle. “To go big. Walk about. Be one of you. She told me it only goes one way, small, and I didn’t believe her. I believe her now.”" ~ fragment
fact frag_sorrel: "“My name,” says Old Sorrel, “was Hester Pell. I blew on the thistle the night Mr. Lowe came with his papers, and I went small, and I have been waiting for someone who would learn the garden properly. I think you have.”" ~ fragment

objective learn: "Learn the garden from the gnomes"
~ done when me.knows.pip_lore and me.knows.sorrel_lore and me.knows.mallow_lore and me.knows.nettle_lore
objective fair: "Win something at the Harvest Fair" ~ done when count {{ ? prizewinning }} >= 1
objective gnomes: "Earn the trust of every gnome" ~ hidden until me.knows.frag_pip ~ done when count(gnome, trust: 3) == 4

(((
Seasons and weather. `on season` fires as the clock enters each one.
)))
on season summer
~ then assert {{ oak in_leaf }}
on season autumn
~ then gain moonbloom_seed when {{ moonbloom seeds }}

(((
Weather: a red sky one month is rain the next. Watch the sky at evening
and you can plan for it.
)))
on turn
~ then retract {{ sky rains }}
~ then assert {{ sky rains }} when {{ sky reddens }}
~ then retract {{ sky reddens }}
~ then roll weather
~ then set offering_taken: no
~ then set nibbled: nothing

(((
`dawn` fires after the NPCs have moved, so the morning can say what the
night did. `turn` fires before them.
)))
on dawn
~ then play morning

table weather: roll d6
~ 1..=3: nothing
~ 4..=6: then assert {{ sky reddens }}

on loud
~ then lower trust with each gnome 1
~ then say sorrel "(from somewhere under the leaves) Must you?"

:: garden_rules [rules]
(((
The garden's rules. A rule tagged `world only` holds in the garden and
never in your almanac; those are the only rules that may say `not`
(stratified: `shaded` is finished before `gets sun` asks about it). Every
other rule holds in the garden always, and in your almanac once known.
)))
rule bed: {{ B soil S }} => {{ B is bed }} ~ known
rule planted: {{ P in B }} and {{ B is bed }} => {{ B has plant }} ~ known
rule suits: {{ P likes S }} and {{ B soil S }} and {{ P wants L }} and {{ B gets L }} => {{ P suits B }} ~ known
rule fed: {{ P in B }} and {{ B composted }} => {{ P fed }} ~ known

rule oak_shade: {{ oak shades B }} and {{ oak in_leaf }} => {{ B shaded }}
rule bell_shade: {{ shadebell in A }} and {{ A beside B }} => {{ B shaded }} ~ learned from sorrel_lore
rule mirror: {{ mirror_lily in A }} and {{ A beside B }} => {{ B lit }} ~ learned from pip_lore
rule thunder: {{ thundercap in A }} and {{ A beside B }} => {{ B watered }} ~ learned from mallow_lore
rule thunder_here: {{ thundercap in B }} => {{ B watered }} ~ learned from mallow_lore

rule rain: {{ sky rains }} and {{ B is bed }} => {{ B watered }} ~ known
rule lit: {{ B lit }} => {{ B gets sun }} ~ known

rule sunny: {{ B is bed }} and not {{ B shaded }} => {{ B gets sun }} ~ world only
rule shady: {{ B shaded }} and not {{ B lit }} => {{ B gets shade }} ~ world only

rule marigold: {{ marigold in A }} and {{ A beside B }} => {{ B guarded }} ~ learned from pip_lore
rule slugs: {{ sky rains }} and {{ B soil clay }} and not {{ B guarded }} => {{ B slugged }} ~ world only

rule thrives: {{ P in B }} and {{ P likes S }} and {{ B soil S }} and {{ P wants L }} and {{ B gets L }} and {{ B watered }} and not {{ B slugged }} => {{ P thrives }} ~ world only
(((
Your almanac's version of `thrives` knows nothing of slugs, because it
cannot say `not`. It is overconfident on purpose: a clay bed in a wet
month will fool it until Old Sorrel teaches you `slug_risk`.
)))

rule hopes: {{ P in B }} and {{ P likes S }} and {{ B soil S }} and {{ P wants L }} and {{ B gets L }} and {{ B watered }} => {{ P thrives }} ~ almanac only ~ known
rule slug_risk: {{ sky rains }} and {{ B soil clay }} and {{ P in B }} => {{ P at_risk }} ~ almanac only ~ learned from sorrel_lore

rule moon: {{ moonbloom in B }} and {{ B blessed }} and {{ B gets shade }} => {{ moonbloom thrives }} ~ learned from nettle_lore

rule cross: {{ mirror_lily in A }} and {{ shadebell in B }} and {{ A beside B }} and {{ mirror_lily thrives }} and {{ shadebell thrives }} => {{ moonbloom seeds }} ~ learned from sorrel_lore
rule prize: {{ P thrives }} and {{ P magical }} => {{ P prizewinning }} ~ learned from fair_notice
rule big: {{ pumpkin thrives }} and {{ pumpkin fed }} => {{ pumpkin prizewinning }} ~ learned from fair_notice

fact mirror_lily_is: "Mirror-lily: a white trumpet that turns the light it catches onto the next bed." ~ means {{ mirror_lily magical }}, {{ mirror_lily likes loam }}, {{ mirror_lily wants sun }}
fact shadebell_is: "Shadebell: a blue bell that drinks the light around it." ~ means {{ shadebell magical }}, {{ shadebell likes clay }}, {{ shadebell wants shade }}
fact thundercap_is: "Thundercap: a grey toadstool that hums before rain." ~ means {{ thundercap magical }}, {{ thundercap likes clay }}, {{ thundercap wants shade }}
fact moonbloom_is: "Moonbloom: born of a mirror-lily and a shadebell flowering side by side. It opens only in a blessed bed, in shade." ~ means {{ moonbloom magical }}

:: cat [archetype]
--
moves: 1 per chronon

goal pounce
~ toward nearest(offering)
~ weight 1
~ when not self.watched
~ then destroy nearest(offering) when not self.watched

:: gnome [archetype]
(((
Gnomes walk at night (the end of each month), and only where nothing is
watching. A lit lantern keeps every gnome off its bed, tenders too: that
is the cost of a lantern.
)))
--
moves: 2 per chronon

goal fetch
~ toward nearest(offering, liked by: self)
~ weight 3
~ when not self.watched
~ then take nearest(offering, liked by: self)
~ then raise trust with self 1
~ then set offering_taken: yes

goal tend
~ toward one(bed, unwatched, where: {{ it has plant }})
~ weight 2
~ when self.is(tender) and trust with self >= 1
~ then assert {{ here watered }}

goal nibble
~ toward one(bed, unwatched, where: {{ it has plant }})
~ weight 2
~ when self.is(nibbler) and trust with self <= 1
~ then set nibbled: one {{ ? in here }}
~ then retract {{ ? in here }}

goal bless
~ toward c
~ weight 4
~ when self.is(sorrel) and trust with self == 3
~ then assert {{ c blessed }}
~ once

reaction teach
~ when trust with self >= 2 and not you.knows(self.lore)
~ then teach you self.lore
~ then teach you self.fragment
~ then give self.gift to you

(((
Mallow can be turned: at trust 2 a nibbler stops nibbling. Nettle turns
last, and only for the silver thimble, which was Hester’s.
)))

:: arrival [prologue]
The letter from the solicitor said *a cottage, an acre, and a garden in some disrepair*. It did not say that the garden would be waiting.

[after 2s]
Thistledown sits at the end of a lane outside Underwick, under a hill. Nine square beds in three rows of three, edged in old brick. All of them weeds, except the middle one, where a single thistle stands taller than a man, silver-headed, perfectly still, as though it had been planted there on purpose and told to wait.

[after 2s]
The gardener before you was Hester Pell. She left last autumn without a word to anyone, and without her almanac, which the solicitor hands you at the gate: a fat book in green cloth, crammed with notes in a small slanting hand, and with half its pages blank.

[note]
A year in nine months: spring, summer, autumn, and the Harvest Fair at the end of it. Six hours of daylight a month. The garden runs on its own rules. Hester’s almanac runs on the ones you know, and on what you have seen with your own eyes. Plant by the almanac. When it’s wrong, you’ve learned something.

[end]
> [[Go into the cottage->cottage]] ~ id arrive

:: cottage
Hester’s cottage: two rooms, a range, a dresser full of jars. Everything clean, as if she meant to come back. The back door opens onto the garden.

[if clock.now == 0]
On the dresser, beside a jam jar and a candle end, someone has left a spoon of honey in a twist of paper, a tin of buttons, and a heel of bread, laid out in a row like offerings on an altar.

[end]
{known about: garden}
> [[Read Hester’s almanac->cottage:almanac]] ~ examine ~ id read_almanac
> [[Make a lantern from the jar and the candle->cottage:lantern]] ~ make lantern ~ requires jam_jar, candle_end ~ id make_lantern
> [[Watch the sky at evening->cottage:sky]] ~ examine ~ id watch_sky
> [[Sleep until next month->here]] ~ pass ~ id sleep

:: cottage:almanac [act]
--
The almanac is half a garden diary and half a book of rules. *Rain waters everything.* *What is lit gets the sun.* *A plant thrives in the soil it likes and the light it wants, if it is watered.* The margins are full of the same small sketch, again and again: a little figure with a pointed hat, peering round a cabbage.

Some pages have been torn out. The stubs are still there.

:: cottage:lantern [act]
--
A jar, the candle end, and a bit of wire off the dresser for a handle. It is a poor thing. When it’s lit, though, it throws a warm round light on whatever it stands beside, and keeps watch there all night.

:: cottage:sky [act]
learn red_sky when {{ sky reddens }}
--
[if {{ sky reddens }}]
The sky over the hill goes red as a coal. Hester’s almanac has a note for that, underlined twice.

[else]
A pale sky, going grey. Nothing to read in it.

:: bed_work [lib]
(((
Every bed embeds this. `here` is the bed.
)))
> [[Look closely at the soil->bed_work:examine]] ~ examine ~ id examine_bed
> [[Watch the light here for an hour->bed_work:observe]] ~ observe ~ id observe_light
> [[Sow something->bed_work:sow]] ~ sow ~ when not {{ here has plant }} ~ id sow
> [[Water it->bed_work:water]] ~ water ~ requires watering_can ~ id water
> [[Leave an offering here->bed_work:leave]] ~ leave ~ when me.holds.any(offering) ~ id leave_offering
> [[Set the lantern here->bed_work:lantern]] ~ leave ~ requires lantern ~ then move lantern to here ~ then set lantern_lit: yes ~ id set_lantern
> [[Dig in compost->bed_work:dig]] ~ dig ~ requires mattock, compost ~ id dig_compost

:: bed_work:examine [act]
learn all {{ here soil ? }}
--
You kneel and crumble a handful.
{known about: here}

:: bed_work:observe [act]
observe {{ here gets ? }}
--
You sit on the brick edge for an hour and watch where the light falls.

[if {{ here gets sun }}]
The sun lies on this bed all afternoon.

[else]
Shade, most of the day.

[end]
(((
`observe` forgets what you knew of `gets` for this bed and learns what is
true now. A bed you watched in spring may have changed by summer, when the
oak comes into leaf. Your almanac will not know until you look again.
)))

:: bed_work:sow [act]
{choose input for: sown, from: me.holds.any(seed)}
let sure: me.knows {{ sown.grows suits here }}
assert {{ sown.grows in here }}
lose sown
raise green_thumb 1 when sure
--
You sow {sown} and firm the soil over it.

[if sure]
Your almanac is sure of this one.

[if me.knows {{ sown.grows at_risk }}; append]
Though Old Sorrel’s note in the margin says to watch for slugs.

[end]

:: bed_work:water [act]
assert {{ here watered }} until next chronon
--
You water it. The can is dented and leaks down your leg.

:: bed_work:leave [act]
{choose input for: gift, from: me.holds.any(offering)}
move gift to here
--
You set {gift} down at the edge of the bed, feeling foolish, and go in.

:: bed_work:lantern [act]
--
You set the lantern down and light it. It watches the bed all night: no cat will come, and nothing else either.

:: bed_work:dig [act]
retract {{ here soil ? }}
assert {{ here soil loam }}
assert {{ here composted }}
--
You break the bed up with Hester’s mattock and fork the compost through it, and it is loam now, black and rich. It is also very loud, and somewhere under the leaves something tuts.

:: nw [bed card:NW]
hint: "Lavender loves this one. So would anything that wants sun and sand." ~ green_thumb ..=2
hint: "It’s the only bed the oak never reaches. Keep the sun-lovers here." ~ green_thumb 3..
--
The north-west bed, under the cottage wall. It drains so fast the weeds look thirsty.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: n [bed card:N]
--
The north bed, in the middle of the top row. A robin watches you from the brick.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: ne [bed card:NE]
hint: "Look at this bed again after midsummer." ~ green_thumb ..=2
hint: "The oak comes into leaf in summer. Sun-lovers here will sulk." ~ green_thumb 3..
--
The north-east bed, near the hedge. Mr. Lowe’s oak leans over it like a neighbour over a fence.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: w [bed card:W]
--
The west bed. Cold clay. Something has been digging small neat holes in the corner.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: c [bed card:C]
hint: "Hester grew one thing here. Ask who tended it." ~ green_thumb ..=2
hint: "The middle bed is the one a gnome would bless. It wants trust, not tools." ~ green_thumb 3..
--
The middle bed. Nothing grows in it but the thistle, which stands taller than you are and does not move in the wind.

[if {{ c blessed }}]
The soil glitters very faintly, as if someone had sown it with frost.

[if me.knows.frag_sorrel]
You know now what the thistle is. You don’t touch it.

[end]
{embed nib: "bed_work"}
> [[Blow on the thistle->ending_small]] ~ when me.knows.frag_sorrel and count(gnome, trust: 3) == 4 ~ id blow_on_the_thistle

:: e [bed card:E]
hint: "Anything here will be in shade come summer." ~ green_thumb ..=2
hint: "Sand and shade, from midsummer on. A thundercap won’t mind the sand." ~ green_thumb 3..
--
The east bed, sandy, by the gap in the hedge. Through the gap you can see a lawn mown in stripes.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: sw [bed card:SW]
--
The south-west bed, beside the potting shed. Clay, and a slug on every leaf after rain.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: s [bed card:S]
--
The south bed, by the back door. The easiest bed in the garden, and the one everyone walks across.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: se [bed card:SE]
--
The south-east bed. Clay that holds the rain, and the oak’s shadow in the afternoons.
{embed nib: "bed_work"}

:: shed
--
The potting shed: cobwebs, flowerpots, a mattock with a handle worn pale by one pair of hands. On the bench, a silver thimble that has no business being in a potting shed.
> [[Take the mattock->shed:mattock]] ~ take mattock ~ id take_mattock
> [[Take the compost barrow->shed:compost]] ~ take compost ~ id take_compost
> [[Take the thimble->shed:thimble]] ~ take thimble ~ id take_thimble

:: shed:mattock [act]
--
It fits your hands as if it had been waiting for them.

:: shed:compost [act]
--
Heavy, and it smells like years.

:: shed:thimble [act]
--
Engraved inside, very small: *H.P.*

:: hedge
--
The gap in the hedge, where the old path went through to the next garden before Mr. Lowe planted his lawn over it.

[if clock.now <= 2]
Mr. Lowe is there, as he is most mornings, in a good tweed coat, looking over his hedge at your weeds with an expression of polite pain. “Mrs. Pell was going to sell to me, you know,” he says. “Before she went off. I made her a very fair offer. I’ll make you the same, after the Fair. When you’ve had your fun.”

[else]
Mr. Lowe’s lawn, striped like a flag. His cat, Marmalade, is asleep on the wall, or pretending to be.

[end]
> [[Ask Mr. Lowe about Hester->hedge:ask]] ~ examine ~ id ask_lowe

:: hedge:ask [act]
--
“A nice old woman. Odd. Talked to her cabbages.” He looks at the middle bed, at the thistle, and something in his face goes careful. “I’d have that thing out if I were you. Seeds everywhere. It’s a weed.”

:: lane
--
The lane to Underwick. A notice is nailed to the gatepost.
> [[Read the notice->lane:notice]] ~ examine ~ id read_notice

:: lane:notice [act]
learn fair_notice
--
THE UNDERWICK HARVEST FAIR. At the end of autumn, on the green. Prizes for any marvel of the garden grown in good health; and for the largest pumpkin, properly fed. Judges’ decision final.

:: morning [interlude]
(((
Played at dawn each month, after the gnomes have been abroad.
)))
[if clock.now in [3, 6]]
It is {clock.season} now.

[if clock.now == 3]
The oak next door has come into full leaf overnight. Its shadow lies across the whole east side of the garden.

[if {{ sky rains }}]
It rains all month. The almanac says that is good for everything.

[if offering_taken]
The offering you left is gone. In its place, in the soil, the print of a very small boot.

[if nibbled != nothing]
Something has been at the {nibbled}. Nibbled to the stalk.

[if any P: me.knows {{ P thrives }} and not {{ P thrives }}]
Your almanac said something here would thrive. It hasn’t. The almanac is wrong, somewhere, and you are going to have to find out where.

[end]
> [[Go out into the garden->s]] ~ id go_out

:: harvest_fair [finale]
(((
Comes when the clock passes the last month, as Ex 1's terminus does.
)))
The Underwick Harvest Fair, on the green, under bunting. Mr. Lowe has entered a pumpkin the size of a sheep.

[if count {{ ? prizewinning }} >= 1]
The judges stop at your table and stay there a long time. One of them takes off his hat.

[else]
The judges pass your table politely, as one passes a neighbour’s new baby.

[end]
> [[Go home to Thistledown->ending_fair]] ~ when count {{ ? prizewinning }} >= 1 ~ id won_the_fair
> [[Go home to Thistledown->ending_sold]] ~ when count {{ ? prizewinning }} == 0 and count(gnome, trust: 3) < 2 ~ id lost_the_fair
> [[Go home to Thistledown->ending_wild]] ~ when count {{ ? prizewinning }} == 0 and count(gnome, trust: 3) >= 2 ~ id the_garden_keeps_you

:: ending_fair [ending]
--
They give you a rosette for the {one {{ ? prizewinning }}}, and the judges write the name of the garden in the book: *Thistledown*. Mr. Lowe shakes your hand and does not make his offer.

[if me.knows.frag_sorrel]
That night you leave the rosette in the middle bed, at the foot of the thistle. In the morning it is gone, and in its place, in the soil, are the prints of four pairs of very small boots, and a fifth pair, a little larger, that walked round and round the thistle as if dancing.

[else]
That night, for no reason you could explain, you leave a spoon of honey in the middle bed.

:: ending_sold [ending]
--
Mr. Lowe makes his offer the week after the Fair. It is very fair. You take it.

On your last morning you go out to the middle bed to say goodbye to the thistle, feeling foolish, and find it has gone to seed overnight: the whole silver head of it blown away, every last seed, over the hedge and onto Mr. Lowe’s striped lawn.

By spring his lawn is all thistles. By summer, his roses are being nibbled to the stalk every night by something nobody ever sees.

:: ending_wild [ending]
--
You win nothing at the Fair, and you don’t mind as much as you thought you would.

The gnomes do not mind at all. Over the winter the beds go back to weeds, and the weeds are full of small paths, and the small paths are full of small lanterns, and on some nights, if you stand at the back door very quietly, you can hear them singing in the middle bed.

Mr. Lowe makes his offer. You tell him the garden isn’t yours to sell. You’re not sure, any more, whose it is.

:: ending_small [ending]
(((
The gnome ending: every gnome's trust, and Old Sorrel's story.
)))
--
You stand in the middle bed with the thistle’s silver head in front of your face, and four small faces watching you from under the leaves, and one of them, the oldest, nods.

You blow.

The thistledown goes up around you like snow falling the wrong way, and the garden gets very large very quickly: the brick edge a wall, the leaves a roof, the thistle stalk a tower. And at the foot of it, in a pointed hat, an old woman with a small slanting smile is holding out a spoon of honey.

“Welcome to Thistledown,” says Hester Pell. “Mind Nettle. She bites.”
